{
  "dom_id": "step-q162326",
  "name": "Question 11",
  "problem": "Evaluate\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y.$\n\n*Hint: First change the order of integration to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.$*",
  "worked_solution": "We wish to find the limits of integration such that our integral can be expressed as \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(y,z)}^{{u}_{2}(y,z)}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z.\n$$\n\nFrom the repeated integral, we see that the domain of integration $R$ can be written as a type II region as follows:\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\,0\\le x\\le \\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\}\n$$\n\nSince all repeated integrals first integrate with respect to $x,$ the $x$-limits remain the same as before:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{{v}_{1}(z)}^{{v}_{2}(z)}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we consider the projection $D$ of $R$ onto the $yz$-plane shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14234808/assets/ed7308cf6c77f5d89b94ec90237fa9161fc85429182813f38b55d916bdbdaeef.png)\n\nThe region $D$ is type I region in the $yz$-plane:\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le y\\le 1,\\,\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTo change the order of integration from $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y$ to $\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z,$ we write $D$ as a type II region.\n\nNotice that the left boundary of the region $D$ is\n\n$$\ny=0\n$$\n\nand the right boundary of the region $D,$ written in the form $y=y(z),$ is\n\n$$\nz=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y\\,⟹\\,y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡z.\n$$\n\nThen, the type II representation of the region is\n\n$$\nD=\\{(y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le y\\le \\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14234808/assets/4fb3323b2c295df8351910890f20266840b131a4dfd8a7bba0d61a3eb03bea23.png)\n\nTherefore, by swapping the order of integration, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡y}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}z\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}[\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\text{d}x]\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,[x]_{0}^{\\operatorname{cos}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}[\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\text{d}y]\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,[y]_{0}^{\\operatorname{sin}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡z\\operatorname{cos}⁡z\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡z}\\,\\text{d}z \\\\  & ={[-\\frac{1}{3}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡z)}^{3/2}]}_{0}^{\\pi /2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}[(1+0)^{3/2}-(1+1)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}(1-{2}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}-1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
